        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Chinese folk religion</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1250 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, from the beginnings of Chinese civilisation: ancestor veneration from prehistory and divination by 1250–1150 BCE; as popular religion distinct from the Three Teachings, primarily from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> (960–1279).</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: it grew from local cults of gods, immortals and ancestors, bound to villages, lineages and temples rather than to a founder or a church.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">none of its own: it draws on a vast heritage of sacred books, from the Confucian classics and the I Ching to the Taoist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daodejing">Daodejing</a>, the precious scrolls (baojuan) of the folk sects, morality books and works of mythology.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Yang and Hu (2012) counted 55.5% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s adults, 578 million people; the China Family Panel Studies’ 2012 survey found 81% of China’s population (1 billion) worshipping gods and ancestors in the traditional way; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> 42.7% of adults (16 million) are self-identified folk believers.</span></div>
