    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Chinese folk religion has no single church or creed: one takes part by belonging to a village, a lineage or a temple association with its gods and rituals, not by believing an official doctrine.</li><li>Its common core is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a>, Heaven, the source of moral meaning; qi, the energy of which all things are made; the veneration of ancestors; and bao ying, moral reciprocity.</li><li>Condemned as “feudal superstition” after 1911 and attacked most of all in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> (1966–1976), it revived after 1978, with millions of temples rebuilt or built from scratch.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Many temples in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> reopened because their gods were said to reclaim them: a Chenghuang Temple in Yulin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, used as a granary in the Cultural Revolution, was restored in the 1980s after the seeds stored in it were always found to have rotted.</p></aside>
    </section>

